WEBVTT

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.290
And because your muscles are voracious energy hogs, each one is rigged up with its own personal

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.890
nerve to stimulate contraction, and its own artery and vein to keep it well-fed with all

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.230
the blood, and oxygen, and nutrients it needs to operate.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.750
But to understand those operations, we first need to get a grip on the anatomy of a skeletal

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.870
muscle, which involves fibers within fibers, and lots of layers.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:21.650
Basically, a skeletal muscle is constructed like a really sturdy piece of rope.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.070
Thousands of tiny, parallel threads called myofibrils squish together to form muscle

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:30.550
fibers, which are your actual muscle cells -- cells with mitochondria, multiple nuclei,

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.110
and a cellular membrane called a sarcolemma.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.790
Those muscle fibers then form larger, string-like bundles called fascicles, which combine to

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.070
form the larger rope-like muscle organ, like your biceps brachii.

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.990
Overall, this bundles-of-bundles configuration makes muscle tissue fairly sturdy. But considering

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.610
how much abuse your muscles take when you do something like pretty simple, like lift

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.730
a big bag of dog food, it’s no surprise they need a little help.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.990
That’s why every muscle contains a few different kinds of supportive sheaths of connective

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.850
tissue -- the protective reinforcements to keep that bulging muscle from bursting.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.070
So that is the structure part of the story.
